AUTHORS = [
A("Charles Dickens", MAJ, "1812", "1870", "Landport, Portsmouth, England",
  "English; the Victorian novel",
  "Novelist, editor, journalist and public reader; the central figure of the Victorian novel and the most widely read English novelist of the nineteenth century.",
  "Born on 7 February 1812 at Landport, Portsmouth, the second child of John Dickens, a navy pay-office clerk, and Elizabeth Barrow. The family's move to London in 1822 and his father's imprisonment for debt in the Marshalsea in 1824 ended his schooling at twelve and put him to work pasting labels at Warren's blacking warehouse, an experience he concealed until late in life and drew on directly in David Copperfield. He was articled to a solicitor in 1827, learned shorthand, and from 1832 reported for the True Sun and the Morning Chronicle, where his Sketches by Boz began to appear in 1833-36. The Pickwick Papers (1836-37) made him famous within months; he married Catherine Hogarth on 2 April 1836, the same month the first number appeared. From 1836 to 1870 he wrote fifteen novels, edited Bentley's Miscellany (1836-39), Master Humphrey's Clock (1840-41), the Daily News (1846) and from 1850 Household Words, continued from 1859 as All the Year Round; he toured America in 1842 and 1867-68, Italy and Switzerland in 1844-45, and from 1858 to 1870 gave public readings of his own work that ruined his health. The separation from Catherine in 1858 and the long attachment to Ellen Ternan belong to the same years as Great Expectations and Our Mutual Friend. He died at Gad's Hill Place, Kent, on 9 June 1870, leaving The Mystery of Edwin Drood unfinished at the sixth of twelve projected numbers; he is buried in Poets' Corner, Westminster Abbey.",
  "Dickens made the serial novel the central literary institution of the Victorian period and gave it a double audience: the family reading aloud the monthly part and the crowded weekly number of Household Words. His plots are built on withheld identity, inheritance, legal chancery and the long orbit of a single city, and his prose moves between a reportorial exactness about institutions, a comic inventiveness of speech and name, and a rhetorical pathos that addresses the reader directly. He wrote as a reformer with specific targets — the workhouse, the Yorkshire schools, Chancery, the Circumlocution Office, the factory system, the prison and the criminal law — and the novels argue by caricature and by the multiplication of institutions into systems. From Bleak House (1852-53) onward the novels take a darker structure: a central secret which the narrative withholds from its protagonist, and a double narrative, first-person and third-person, that measures the cost of knowledge.",
  "Innumerable proper names that carry character as epithet (Pecksniff, Gradgrind, Bounderby, Scrooge, Uriah Heep, Mr F's Aunt); a syntax of accumulating clauses that builds comic or pathetic crescendos; the present-tense opening of Bleak House (\"London. Michaelmas Term lately over\") and the fog that states a legal system; recurring motifs that structure whole novels (the river in Our Mutual Friend, the dust-heaps, the fog, the Marshalsea); an unrivalled ear for the idiolects of class, trade and profession.",
  "The condition of England and the mutual obligations of the classes; childhood as a moral test of social arrangements; the law, the bureaucracy and the institution as machines for the destruction of persons; the criminal and the outcast as the mirror of respectable society; education as training or as repression; money, speculation and the corruption of the will; death, memory and the recovered parent; the city as an organism.",
  "Dickens fixed the form of the English serial novel and its commercial economy, invented or consolidated the Christmas book and the public reading, and made novel-writing a platform for social investigation that led directly to reform (the Sanitary Act, the reformatory schools, the abolition of the Court of Chancery's abuses). His example shaped Gaskell, Collins, Reade, Dostoevsky, Kafka, and the whole tradition of the novel as an indictment. In the twentieth century his reputation was remade by Leavisite and later by post-structuralist criticism, and he is now read with Trollope as the double centre of the Victorian novel.",
  infl=["Henry Fielding", "Tobias Smollett", "Laurence Sterne", "Walter Scott", "William Shakespeare"],
  alt=["Boz", "Charles John Huffam Dickens"],
  status="complete"),

A("William Makepeace Thackeray", SEC, "1811", "1863", "Calcutta, British India",
  "English; the Victorian novel and periodical satire",
  "Novelist, satirist, travel writer and periodical essayist; the great comic-satirical novelist of the 1840s and 1850s and Dickens's chief rival in the monthly-part market.",
  "Born in Calcutta on 18 July 1811, the only child of Richmond Thackeray, a revenue official of the East India Company, and Anne Becher; he was sent to England in 1817 and educated at Charterhouse and Trinity College, Cambridge, which he left without a degree in 1830. An inheritance from his father was lost in part through the failure of two Indian banks (1834) and in part through his own gambling and the collapse of a newspaper he had financed; he read law at the Middle Temple, and in 1836 married Isabella Shawe, after which he turned to journalism to support a growing family. He was Paris correspondent and then part-owner of the Constitutional, and from 1837 contributed to Fraser's Magazine, the New Monthly Magazine and Punch (from 1842), where the Yellowplush Papers, The Book of Snobs (1846-47) and the Snob Papers established the persona of a moralizing dandy. Vanity Fair appeared in monthly parts in 1847-48 and made him famous; from 1851 he lectured in England and in America on the English humourists and the Georges. He stood unsuccessfully for Parliament in 1857 and edited the Cornhill Magazine from 1860, where his last completed novels were serialised. He died in London on 24 December 1863.",
  "Thackeray wrote the Victorian novel against the Victorian novel: his narrators are intrusive, worldly, digressive and self-accusing, and they refuse the consolations that Dickens's plots allow. His subject is the machinery of social self-presentation — the snob, the adventuress, the place-seeker, the respectable man who has quietly ruined himself — and his method is a cool retrospective narration that knows the ending from the first page and passes judgement while withholding it. He is a historian of manners by instinct: Esmond and The Virginians reconstruct the eighteenth century from its newspapers, its memoirs and its genealogies, and Henry Esmond is the first English novel to be narrated throughout in the first person by a man of a past age in that age's own idiom.",
  "Retrospective narration and the extended aside; irony so even that sympathy and contempt are deliberately indistinguishable; chapter titles and epigraphs that moralise in miniature; an exact notation of small social transactions (a bow, a dinner, a debt); the rejection of coincidence and of the heroic climax; a prose that carries the rhythms of the eighteenth-century essayists.",
  "Snobbery and the fear of social shame; vanity and the market in reputation; marriage as a commercial transaction; the gentleman as a problem in morals rather than in birth; the illusions of family history; the corruption of politics and of the press; the vanity of human wishes, in a register nearer Ecclesiastes than to satire.",
  "Vanity Fair is the most complete English novel of the 1840s about the 1840s, and the phrase itself passed into the language as the name of the world of display. With Dickens he established the monthly-part novel as the dominant cultural form of the decade. His historical novels taught the nineteenth century how to imagine the eighteenth, and his lectures and Cornhill editorship made him an arbiter of the literary profession. Trollope, Meredith, Wilde and the whole line of the novel of manners descend from him.",
  infl=["Henry Fielding", "Jonathan Swift", "Laurence Sterne", "William Makepeace Thackeray (as essayist)", "Horace Walpole"],
  alt=["W. M. Thackeray", "Michael Angelo Titmarsh", "William Makepeace Thackeray"],
  status="complete"),

A("Charlotte Brontë", MAJ, "1816", "1855", "Thornton, Bradford, Yorkshire, England",
  "English; the Victorian novel, Yorkshire",
  "Novelist and poet; the author of Jane Eyre and the first of the Brontë sisters to publish, and the one who turned the provincial parsonage into a literary subject.",
  "Born at Thornton on 21 April 1816, the third daughter of Patrick Brontë, perpetual curate, and Maria Branwell; the family moved to Haworth in 1820, and her mother died in 1821. She and her sisters were sent in 1824 to the Clergy Daughters' School at Cowan Bridge, the model for Lowood in Jane Eyre, where her two elder sisters Maria and Elizabeth died in 1825. She taught at Roe Head (1835-38) and was twice a governess (1839, 1841); in 1842 she and Emily went to the Pensionnat Heger in Brussels to qualify to open a school of their own, and her unreturned attachment to Constantin Heger produced the letters she wrote to him in 1844-45. The school project failed. Poems by Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell (1846) sold two copies; Jane Eyre appeared on 16 October 1847 and was an immediate success. She nursed Emily and Anne through their deaths in 1848-49, published Shirley in 1849 and Villette in 1853, and married Arthur Bell Nicholls, her father's curate, on 29 June 1854. She died at Haworth on 31 March 1855, in the first year of her marriage; her first novel, The Professor, was published posthumously in 1857.",
  "Charlotte Brontë wrote the first English novel whose heroine claims the right to feel and to speak in her own person, and Jane Eyre's first-person narrative gives the Victorian novel its most sustained study of the interior life of an unregarded woman. Her novels work by a strict first-person confinement: the reader knows only what the narrator has experienced and can articulate. She is a writer of Gothic intensity inside a realist frame, and her landscapes and weather are states of feeling. Shirley turns from the private to the historical (the Luddite risings of 1811-12), Villette to the isolation of the Englishwoman abroad, and the late novels deliberately refuse the marriage-plot consolations that Jane Eyre permits.",
  "Intense first-person narration, often retrospective and addressed to an unnamed reader; short, abrupt paragraphs and direct address; dream and vision sequences used as psychological evidence, not as supernatural event; dialect speech (Joseph, Emily Brontë's Yorkshire voices) used as a moral register; a symbolic use of weather and landscape; the plain hero and the plain heroine, both ugly by convention and both proud of it.",
  "The rights of the feeling subject against social and economic power; the governess and the class position of educated women without money; the relation of the passions to religious vocation; the struggle of the will (Rochester's, Paul Emanuel's, Lucy Snowe's) with love; madness, inheritance and the colonial fortune; the family as a closed and often destructive system.",
  "Jane Eyre changed what an English novel could say about a woman's inner life, and the phrase 'the reader, I married him' style of direct address became a permanent resource of first-person fiction. Her example (with Emily's and Anne's) made the Brontë parsonage a central site of English literary history, and the twentieth-century feminist recovery of the Brontës made her work the starting point for the criticism of women's writing. Wide Sargasso Sea (1966), Jean Rhys's reply to Jane Eyre, is the most famous postcolonial rewriting of a Victorian novel.",
  infl=["Lord Byron", "Walter Scott", "William Shakespeare", "Samuel Taylor Coleridge", "John Milton"],
  alt=["Currer Bell"],
  pseudo=["Currer Bell"],
  unc="The authorship of the three 'Bell' novels was from 1846 concealed behind pseudonyms, and Charlotte's later editorial work on her sisters' texts (1850) altered Emily's poems; the early letters survive only in copies made by Ellen Nussey."),

A("Emily Brontë", MAJ, "1818", "1848", "Thornton, Bradford, Yorkshire, England",
  "English; the Victorian novel and lyric poetry",
  "Novelist and poet; the author of Wuthering Heights, and the most original and least documented of the Brontë sisters.",
  "Born at Thornton on 30 July 1818, the fifth child of Patrick Brontë; she grew up at Haworth and, apart from a few months at Roe Head (1835) and the year at the Pensionnat Heger in Brussels (1842-43), never left the parsonage and the moors. She kept the Gondal chronicle, a verse and prose saga of an imaginary north Pacific kingdom, with Anne from childhood, and her poems and Anne's were included in the privately financed Poems by Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell (1846). Wuthering Heights was published in December 1847 in three volumes together with Anne's Agnes Grey; the reviewers were largely hostile or puzzled, and the novel sold poorly. Her health declined through the autumn of 1848, and she refused medical treatment, dying at Haworth on 19 December 1848. Her poems were edited with a memoir by Charlotte in 1850.",
  "Emily Brontë wrote one novel and a body of lyric and Gondal poems, and no other English writer of comparable standing is so entirely without a biography. Wuthering Heights refuses the moral and formal conventions of the Victorian novel: it is narrated at two removes, by the outsider Lockwood and the servant Nelly Dean, whose accounts disagree; its structure is that of a chronicle extending over three generations; and the passion at its centre exceeds both narrators' comprehension. The poems are hard, short, stoical and impersonal, and the finest of them ('No coward soul is mine', 'The Prisoner', 'Remembrance') have the accent of the border ballad and of the hymn rather than of the Romantic lyric.",
  "A frame-within-a-frame narrative with two partial and unreliable narrators; a dialect voice (Joseph) used as a barrier to comprehension; violent abrupt transitions and a refusal of explanation; a prose that is spare in description and dense in the notation of weather, stone and animal life; in the poems, short ballad measure, abrupt syntax, and a deliberate coldness of tone even at the height of grief.",
  "The passion that exceeds social form; the relation of the self to place and to the dead; revenge and inheritance across two generations; the Calvinist inheritance and its alternatives (Nelly's moralism, Joseph's fanaticism, Catherine's dream of heaven); class and property in a moorland yeoman economy; the supernatural as a fact of psychology, not a device of plot.",
  "Wuthering Heights is the one Victorian novel that has never settled into the Victorian canon, and each generation has remade it (as a Gothic romance, a Marxist study of property, a feminist account of the female will, a postcolonial reading of Heathcliff's origin). Its double narration is a permanent model for the unreliable frame, and the Gondal poems are a central document in the debate about poetic imagination as a private system.",
  infl=["George Gordon, Lord Byron", "Walter Scott", "Johann Wolfgang von Goethe", "John Milton", "Emanuel Swedenborg (by family tradition)"],
  alt=["Ellis Bell"],
  pseudo=["Ellis Bell"],
  unc="Almost nothing is documented of her life: no letters survive in her own hand except two notes, and the Gondal prose chronicles were lost. Charlotte's 1850 memoir and her editing of the poems are the source of most later accounts and are themselves contested; the standard evidence is Winifred Gérin's biography (1971) and the Clarendon edition of the poems."),

A("Anne Brontë", SEC, "1820", "1849", "Thornton, Bradford, Yorkshire, England",
  "English; the Victorian novel and hymn",
  "Novelist and poet; the youngest Brontë sister and the most openly reforming of the three novelists.",
  "Born at Thornton on 17 January 1820, the sixth and last child of Patrick Brontë. She was educated at home and at Roe Head (1835-37), and from 1839 worked as a governess, at Blake Hall (1839) and for four years (1840-45) at Thorpe Green, the seat of the Robinson family, an experience she used directly in Agnes Grey. With Emily she kept the Gondal chronicle, and her poems are in the 1846 volume. Agnes Grey was published with Wuthering Heights in December 1847, and The Tenant of Wildfell Hall in June 1848, to a reception that included Charlotte's disapproval of its subject. She died at Scarborough on 28 May 1849, where she had gone for her health, and is buried there rather than at Haworth.",
  "Anne Brontë wrote two novels and a body of hymns and Gondal poems, and she is the Brontë who writes most directly as a moralist and least as a romantic. Agnes Grey is a short, exact and unsentimental account of the governess's position, drawn from her own employment; The Tenant of Wildfell Hall is the first English novel to describe a wife leaving a drunken and abusive husband and to defend the act on religious and moral grounds. Both are written in the plain style, and both use a strict first-person or epistolary frame to make the recounting of suffering a matter of testimony rather than of sensation.",
  "Plain declarative prose; a documentary exactness about wages, households and routines; diary and letter forms used as evidence; a direct homiletic address to the reader; deliberate refusal of the Gothic machinery that her sisters used.",
  "The governess's position between the family and the servant class; the wife's legal non-existence under coverture; the moral effects of drink and of fashionable education; the religious duty of truth-telling; the education of children as a test of character.",
  "The Tenant of Wildfell Hall is now read as one of the founding texts of Victorian feminist argument, and its frank treatment of alcoholism, marital cruelty and the law of custody was deliberately confrontational in 1848. Its neglect and Charlotte's judgement of it ('hardly ... desirable') kept it out of the Brontë canon for a century, and its modern recovery is one of the clearest cases of the critical reversal of a Victorian woman writer's reputation.",
  infl=["Isaac Watts (hymnody)", "John Wesley (hymnody)", "Walter Scott", "Jane Austen"],
  alt=["Acton Bell"],
  pseudo=["Acton Bell"]),

A("George Eliot", MAJ, "1819", "1880", "Nuneaton, Warwickshire, England",
  "English; the Victorian novel and periodical criticism",
  "Novelist, translator, editor and critic, born Mary Ann Evans; the most philosophically ambitious of the Victorian novelists and the one who made realism a conscious literary programme.",
  "Born on 22 November 1819 at South Farm, Arbury, the daughter of Robert Evans, agent to the Newdigate estate; she was educated at boarding schools in Attleborough and Coventry and was converted to evangelical religion in 1832 and away from it, under the influence of Charles and Cara Bray, by 1841. She translated Strauss's Das Leben Jesu (1846) and Feuerbach's Das Wesen des Christentums (1854), edited the Westminster Review from 1851 to 1854, and in 1854 left England with George Henry Lewes, who could not divorce his wife; their union, unlegalised, lasted until his death in 1878, and they lived as husband and wife in London and at The Priory, Regent's Park. Scenes of Clerical Life appeared in Blackwood's Magazine in 1857 under the pseudonym George Eliot; Adam Bede (1859) was an immediate success, and the pseudonym was dropped in 1860. She married John Walter Cross, twenty years her junior, in May 1880, and died in London on 22 December 1880.",
  "Eliot brought to the English novel a philosophical seriousness formed by German idealism, Comtean positivism, Feuerbachian humanism and the new sciences of heredity and social causation. Her realism is not a technique of description but a moral position: the claim that ordinary lives and ordinary provincial places are worthy of art, and that judgement must be suspended while the web of circumstance and inheritance is shown. From Adam Bede through The Mill on the Floss, Silas Marner and Middlemarch to Daniel Deronda her novels widen from the parish to the nation, and their plots turn on the conflict between a vocation and the claims of ordinary happiness. She is the first English novelist to build a novel on the principle of multiple centres of consciousness, and the narratorial voice — ironic, aphoristic and compassionate — is her central instrument.",
  "Aphoristic and digressive narration that addresses the reader as a moral equal; free indirect style used to enter many minds without losing critical distance; a 'web' of parallel and counterpointed plots; a careful notation of dialect, provincial speech and class position; a use of symbols that are natural rather than Gothic (the river, the loom, the stone pit, the portrait); chapter epigraphs.",
  "Sympathy as the basis of ethics; the pressure of the past and of inherited circumstance on the will; vocation and the claims of the affection; women's education and the waste of female intelligence; the movement of religious feeling into secular moral commitment; the individual's relation to the community; the limits of knowledge, in other minds and in history.",
  "Middlemarch is generally taken as the greatest of the Victorian novels and the fullest statement of Victorian liberal realism; Eliot's translation and editorial work carried German thought into English intellectual life, and her criticism shaped the period's ideas of realism and of the novel's moral office. She set the terms for the twentieth-century novel of consciousness (James, Woolf, Proust all read her closely) and for the modern criticism of sympathy in fiction.",
  infl=["William Wordsworth", "Walter Scott", "Jane Austen", "Honoré de Balzac", "Ludwig Feuerbach", "Auguste Comte", "Baruch Spinoza"],
  alt=["Mary Ann Evans", "Mary Anne Evans", "Marian Evans", "George Eliot"],
  pseudo=["George Eliot"],
  status="complete"),

A("Elizabeth Gaskell", SEC, "1810", "1865", "Chelsea, London, England",
  "English; the Victorian novel and social reportage",
  "Novelist and biographer, born Elizabeth Cleghorn Stevenson; the great novelist of the industrial north and of the rural village, and the first biographer of Charlotte Brontë.",
  "Born in Chelsea on 29 September 1810, the daughter of William Stevenson, a Unitarian minister turned government clerk, and Elizabeth Holland; her mother died a month after her birth, and she was brought up by an aunt at Knutsford in Cheshire, the Cranford of her fiction. In 1832 she married William Gaskell, a Unitarian minister in Manchester, where she spent the rest of her life and came to know the poverty of the mill districts at first hand. Her first son died in infancy in 1833 and she wrote her first novel, Mary Barton, in the aftermath of his death; it appeared anonymously in October 1848 and was attacked as a one-sided account of the Manchester strikes. Cranford was serialised in Household Words in 1851-53; North and South in 1854-55; The Life of Charlotte Brontë (1857), commissioned by Patrick Brontë, was attacked by Mrs Brontë's friends and had to be revised. She died suddenly at Holybourne, Hampshire, on 12 November 1865, leaving Wives and Daughters unfinished; it was printed complete with the editor's note in the Cornhill in 1866.",
  "Gaskell wrote from the two vantage points of the Manchester parsonage and the Cheshire village, and her novels hold industrial investigation and domestic comedy in a single frame. Mary Barton and North and South are condition-of-England novels that take the wage, the strike and the masters' blindness as central conflicts; Cranford and the shorter tales make a comic art out of small economies and the claims of gentlewomen without money; Ruth (1853) treats the seduced and abandoned woman without the punitive ending the convention required. Her prose is mobile and observational, she is a superb ventriloquist of different registers of speech, and she works best in the short and middle-length forms where incident accumulates rather than a single plot resolves.",
  "Concrete notation of household economy (what a servant earns, what a dress costs, how a fire is laid); rapid shifts between comedy and pathos within a chapter; a strong sense of the local speech of Lancashire and Cheshire; use of letters and reported conversation as the main instruments of character; an unobtrusive but steady moral commentary on social duty.",
  "The relation of masters and men in industrial conflict; the social position of women without money or protection; the ethics of sympathy across class; the claims of the past and of traditional community; religious conscience (Unitarian and Anglican) and its social expression; illness and death in the household.",
  "Mary Barton was among the first English novels to describe the mill-worker's household and the strike from within, and it shaped the whole condition-of-England tradition from Dickens's Hard Times to the twentieth-century industrial novel. The Life of Charlotte Brontë (1857) is the founding work of Victorian literary biography. Cranford has kept a permanent place as the finest English comic-idyllic fiction of the century, and the late Wives and Daughters is now read as one of the greatest of Victorian novels.",
  infl=["Jane Austen", "Maria Edgeworth", "Mary Wollstonecraft", "William Wordsworth", "Thomas Carlyle"],
  alt=["Mrs Gaskell", "Elizabeth Cleghorn Gaskell", "Elizabeth Stevenson"]),

A("Anthony Trollope", SEC, "1815", "1882", "London, England",
  "English; the Victorian novel, Barsetshire and Westminster",
  "Novelist of the church, the civil service and Parliament; the most prolific and the most systematic of the great Victorian realists, with forty-seven novels, five travel books and two short-story collections.",
  "Born in London on 24 April 1815, the fourth son of Thomas Anthony Trollope, a Chancery barrister whose practice failed, and Frances Milton Trollope, the novelist and travel writer. The family collapsed financially and moved to Harrow Weald and then to a farm near Bruges; Trollope was educated at Winchester and Harrow as a day boy and was miserable at both. He obtained a clerkship in the General Post Office in 1834 and was sent to Ireland as a surveyor's clerk in 1841, where he married Rose Heseltine in 1844 and wrote his first two novels. He invented the pillar box (1851-52) and rose to surveyor-general; from 1859 he lived in London and edited the Fortnightly Review (1865-67) and later Saint Pauls. He stood unsuccessfully for Parliament as a Liberal in 1868 and for a second time in 1874. The Warden (1855) began the Barsetshire series; The Last Chronicle of Barset closed it in 1867; the Palliser novels ran from Can You Forgive Her? (1864) to The Duke's Children (1879-80). He travelled constantly and died in London on 6 December 1882; his posthumously published Autobiography (1883) scandalised the age by stating exactly what he earned.",
  "Trollope wrote fiction as a daily trade, 250 words every quarter of an hour before breakfast, and his art is built on that regularity: he produces a stable fictional world (the county of Barsetshire, the political London of the Pallisers) in which the same institutions — the bishop's palace, the county set, the Government office, the hunting field — recur, and in which character is established by habitual action and by conversation rather than by crisis. His plots are unashamedly about money, marriage and place, and he is the least sentimental of Victorian novelists about the marriage market, clerical ambition and political careerism. He is also an unusually explicit narrator, commenting on his own characters' motives in a tone of tolerant worldly wisdom.",
  "Multiple parallel plots in one book, with a slow rotation of attention; a narrator who addresses the reader as a co-resident of the fictional county; exact notation of income, debts, preferment and social obligation; dialogue that carries class and profession; a refusal of violent incident; long-distance management of dozens of characters over decades.",
  "Ambition, preferment and the machinery of institutional life; the marriage market and the economics of the genteel poor; the Church of England as a career; the relation of private vice to public position; hunting, sport and the rituals of county society; the compromises of political liberalism.",
  "Trollope mapped the professions of Victorian England as no other novelist did, and the Barsetshire and Palliser series are now read as the most complete single portrait of Victorian institutional life. His Autobiography, with its exact accounts of earnings and working method, made authorship a profession in the public eye. He was depreciated by Henry James and the aesthetes, rehabilitated by the twentieth-century critics, and has held a central place in the canon of the realist novel since.",
  infl=["Jane Austen", "William Makepeace Thackeray", "Henry Fielding", "Walter Scott", "Maria Edgeworth"],
  alt=["Anthony Trollope"],
  status="complete"),
]
